@startuml EUDI Security Architecture

!define RECTANGLE class
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE

title EUDI Wallet - Defense in Depth Security Architecture

package "Layer 5: Zero-Trust Verification" #FFE0E0 {
    component [Verify Every Operation] as L5_Verify
    note right of L5_Verify
        • Validate key existence before signing
        • Verify credential expiry on every query
        • Check JWT signatures
        • Validate issuer metadata
    end note
}

package "Layer 4: Storage Encryption" #FFECB3 {
    component [Hardware-Backed Key Storage] as L4_Hardware
    component [Database Encryption] as L4_DB

    note bottom of L4_Hardware
        **iOS:** Secure Enclave (A7+), Keychain
        **Android:** StrongBox (Titan M), KeyStore TEE
        **Properties:**
        • Private keys never leave secure hardware
        • AES-256-GCM encryption
        • Biometric protection available
        • Key accessible only when unlocked
    end note
}

package "Layer 3: Data Validation" #E1F5FE {
    component [Input Validation] as L3_Input
    component [Business Logic Validation] as L3_Business
    component [SQL Injection Prevention] as L3_SQL
}

package "Layer 2: Encryption in Transit" #E8F5E9 {
    component [HTTPS Enforcement] as L2_HTTPS
    component [Certificate Pinning] as L2_Pinning

    note bottom of L2_HTTPS
        **Transport Security**
        • All APIs use HTTPS (TLS 1.2+)
        • Certificate validation enabled
        • No mixed content or HTTP fallback
        • Modern TLS ciphers only
    end note
}

package "Layer 1: Protocol Security" #F3E5F5 {
    component [PKCE (RFC 7636)] as L1_PKCE
    component [Proof of Possession] as L1_PoP
    component [Selective Disclosure] as L1_SD

    note bottom of L1_PKCE
        **Standards Compliance**
        • OpenID4VCI 1.0
        • OpenID4VP 1.0
        • SD-JWT draft-07
        • ICAO 9303 (BAC)
    end note
}

' Relationships showing layered defense
L5_Verify -down-> L4_Hardware : validates
L5_Verify -down-> L4_DB : validates

L4_Hardware -down-> L3_Input : protects
L4_DB -down-> L3_Business : protects

L3_Input -down-> L2_HTTPS : secured by
L3_Business -down-> L2_HTTPS : secured by
L3_SQL -down-> L2_HTTPS : secured by

L2_HTTPS -down-> L1_PKCE : uses
L2_Pinning -down-> L1_PoP : validates

' Attack surface mapping
cloud "Attack Surfaces" as Attacks {
    [Credential Theft\nMitigation: Hardware-backed keys]
    [Man-in-the-Middle\nMitigation: HTTPS + Pinning]
    [Replay Attacks\nMitigation: Nonces + KB-JWT]
    [SQL Injection\nMitigation: Parameterized queries]
    [Data Leakage\nMitigation: Selective disclosure]
}

Attacks .up.> L5_Verify : mitigated by all layers
Attacks .up.> L1_SD

legend bottom
    **Security Principles**
    • Defense in Depth (5 layers)
    • Zero-Trust Architecture
    • Privacy by Design
    • Standards Compliance
    • Hardware Security

    **Threat Model Coverage**
    ✓ Credential theft
    ✓ Man-in-the-middle
    ✓ Replay attacks
    ✓ SQL injection
    ✓ Data leakage
    ✓ Code interception (PKCE)
    ✓ Unauthorized disclosure
endlegend

@enduml
